# Converting GitHub App Private Key to PKCS8 Format
# ==================================================
#
# This file is a PKCS8-formatted version of the GitHub App private key.
# Jenkins/Java sometimes requires PKCS8 format instead of the standard PEM format.
#
# How to convert the original .pem to .pem.PKCS8:
# ------------------------------------------------
#
# The original .pem file downloaded from GitHub is in PKCS1 format.
# To convert it to PKCS8 format, use the following OpenSSL command:
#
# openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -inform PEM -outform PEM -nocrypt \
#   -in jenkins-ci.2025-10-24.private-key.pem \
#   -out jenkins-ci.2025-10-24.private-key.pem.PKCS8
#
# Explanation of the command:
# ---------------------------
# - pkcs8: Use the PKCS8 conversion utility
# - -topk8: Convert to PKCS8 format
# - -inform PEM: Input file is in PEM format
# - -outform PEM: Output file should be in PEM format
# - -nocrypt: Don't encrypt the output (no passphrase)
# - -in: Input file (the original .pem from GitHub)
# - -out: Output file (the PKCS8 version)
#
# When to use which format:
# -------------------------
# - Use the original .pem if Jenkins accepts it
# - Use the .pem.PKCS8 if you get errors like:
#   * "invalid key format"
#   * "Could not parse private key"
#   * Java-related key parsing errors
#
# In your Jenkins JCasC configuration (jenkins.yaml), reference the file that works:
#   privateKey: "${readFile:/run/secrets/jenkins-ci.2025-10-24.private-key.pem.PKCS8}"
#
# IMPORTANT:
# ----------
# - Both files contain the same key, just in different formats
# - Keep both files in secrets/ directory
# - NEVER commit either file to Git (they're in .gitignore)
# - If you regenerate the GitHub App key, you'll need to convert the new one too
